of eight characters. `\u00fa` is not on it, so `N\u00da\u00d1EZ` never matched `Nunez`. Any accented letter outside the list, such as `\u00e7`, `\u00f8` or `\u0151`, would fail the same way.\n2. **Mac vs. Windows encoding.** macOS typically sends names in decomposed form (`e` + combining acute, NFD). Windows sends precomposed characters (NFC). The old code compared the raw strings, so `\u00e9` in NFD never matched `\u00e9` in NFC. The old table only covered precomposed characters.\n3. **German `\u00df`.** `str.lower()` leaves `\u00df` alone, so `Gro\u00df` never matched `Gross`. `casefold()` maps `\u00df` to `ss`.\n\n**Fix** (`contacts/dedupe.py`)\n\n- `name_key` now decomposes with NFKD and drops combining marks, which handles any accented letter in either encoding. It then collapses whitespace and calls `casefold()`.\n- `email_key` uses NFKC and `casefold()` so it follows the same case-insensitive rule as before, with the same Unicode normalization.\n- The hand-written accent table is removed.\n- `dedupe` is unchanged, so the first-seen record still survives.\n\n**Verification**\n\n- `tests/test_dedupe.py::test_accent_variants_merge` passes. I ran it through the pytest on your PATH because `python3 -I` doesn't see pytest.\n- An ad-hoc check confirmed these behaviors:\n  - An NFD `Jos\u00e9 N\u00fa\u00f1ez` merges with a precomposed `Jos\u00e9 N\u00fa\u00f1ez`.\n  - `Jurgen Gross` merges with `J\u00fcrgen Gro\u00df`.\n  - An email differing only in case is dropped.\n  - `JUERGEN GROSS` is kept separate. Transliterating `\u00fc` to `ue` is a different convention, so I left it alone.\n\nI did not commit anything.", "ttft_ms": 1157, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 20212, "uuid": "2bb6007c-02e5-4ddb-b879-92966e82ef8d", "ttft_stream_ms": 956, "time_to_request_ms": 112, "first_content_frame_ms": 956, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T15:49:54.965Z"}